# What this layer needs on the machine for its own setup scripts to run, in the same six
# fields a module uses, name | kind | locator | policy | consumer | reason.
#
# This layer is a declarer like any other. It owns DEPENDENCIES.map and the Brewfile, but the
# tools its own scripts call are not exempt from being declared, otherwise they are the one
# category nothing checks. Read by src/check-dependencies.sh alongside every module manifest.
xcode-select | system | /usr/bin/xcode-select | required | install-xcode-clt.sh | raising Apple's installer when the headless install cannot finish
sw_vers      | system | /usr/bin/sw_vers      | required | install-xcode-clt.sh | reading the running macOS major version, which names the SDK the command line tools must carry
softwareupdate | system | /usr/sbin/softwareupdate | required | install-xcode-clt.sh | finding and installing the command line tools for this macOS without a dialog
sudo         | system | /usr/bin/sudo         | required | setup.sh, install-xcode-clt.sh, setup-kanata.sh | asking for the password once and keeping it for the run, installing the command line tools and the Karabiner driver, and loading the kanata daemons, which only root may do
brew         | path   | brew                  | required | install-homebrew-packages.sh, setup-kanata.sh | every package install in this repository runs through it, and holding kanata with a pin
stow         | path   | stow                  | required | setup-stow-dotfiles.sh | symlinking every module into the home directory
git          | path   | git                   | required | install-ohmyzsh-plugins.sh, bootstrap-nvim.sh | cloning the framework and its plugins, and reading each installed plugin's revision to compare it against the lockfile
duti         | path   | duti                  | required | set-dev-defaults.sh | setting the default application for development file types
jq           | path   | jq                    | required | setup-claude-settings.sh, bootstrap-nvim.sh, install-homebrew-packages.sh, setup-kanata.sh | merging the status line key into the Claude settings without losing the others, reading the names and commits out of lazy-lock.json, reading which app each cask installs, and reading Homebrew's stable kanata for an upgrade
curl         | system | /usr/bin/curl         | required | install-ohmyzsh.sh, install-claude-code.sh, setup-kanata.sh | fetching the upstream installers, and the Karabiner driver package
herdr        | path   | herdr                 | optional | setup-herdr-plugins.sh | registering the local plugin that stow places but cannot register, the popups are nameless without it
installer    | system | /usr/sbin/installer   | required | setup-kanata.sh | installing the Karabiner driver package, which no package manager here covers
pkgutil      | system | /usr/sbin/pkgutil     | required | setup-kanata.sh | reading which driver version is installed and checking the package's notarization and developer team
shasum       | system | /usr/bin/shasum       | required | setup-kanata.sh | checking the driver package against the hash pinned in dotfiles/kanata/DRIVER, and noticing a changed kanata config
launchctl    | system | /bin/launchctl        | required | setup-kanata.sh | loading and restarting the kanata and driver daemons
systemextensionsctl | system | /usr/bin/systemextensionsctl | required | setup-kanata.sh, machine.sh | reading whether the driver extension is activated, which needs no privilege
defaults     | system | /usr/bin/defaults     | required | setup-macos-shortcuts.sh | reading and writing the system's keyboard shortcuts domain as the running session holds it
plutil       | system | /usr/bin/plutil       | required | setup-macos-shortcuts.sh | reading and changing the one flag of a shortcut in the exported domain without touching any other
activateSettings | system | /System/Library/PrivateFrameworks/SystemAdministration.framework/Resources/activateSettings | optional | setup-macos-shortcuts.sh | telling the running session to read the shortcuts again, without which a change waits for the next login
open         | system | /usr/bin/open         | optional | setup-grants.sh, open-in-terminal.sh | opening the pane where a grant is given, revealing the program to add, and opening an application so it can answer for its own grant, without which a grant is only a closing note, and opening a Terminal window for a step a person has to sit through
